; `f.round` AT EXACTLY `.5`, which is the only input where the five implementations disagreed.
;
; They agreed on every other value, which is why nothing caught it: `round(2.4)` and `round(2.6)`
; are 2 and 3 whatever rule you use. The tie is the whole test.
;
;   VM / jvm / swift   math.rint / .rounded(.toNearestOrEven)   half to EVEN
;   js                 Math.round                               half UP          2.5 -> 3
;   rust               .round()                                 half AWAY        -2.5 -> -3
;
; The Core IR contract is HALF TO EVEN (10-core-ir.md), so js and rust were changed to match the
; other three rather than the other way round — three of five already did it, it is what
; `math.rint` and IEEE-754's default rounding mean, and v3 had already shipped parity probes
; pinning `round(2.5) = 2` on two lanes that both run the VM.
;
; `-0.5` IS IN HERE ON PURPOSE, and what it pins is the TIE, not the sign of the zero: half to even
; sends it to zero while half-away-from-zero sends it to -1. The printed form does not distinguish
; -0.0 from 0.0 on any of these lanes — measured, the VM prints `0` — so this row cannot be read as
; a statement about signed zero.
;
; Expected output, from the VM (the oracle check.sh compares every backend against), COPIED FROM A
; RUN rather than predicted — the floats print without a trailing `.0` here:
;   2
;   4
;   -2
;   -4
;   2
;   3
;   0
(program
 (defs
  (def main
   (lam 0
    (seq
     (prim __autoOutput__ (prim f.round (lit (float 2.5))))
     (seq
      (prim __autoOutput__ (prim f.round (lit (float 3.5))))
      (seq
       (prim __autoOutput__ (prim f.round (lit (float -2.5))))
       (seq
        (prim __autoOutput__ (prim f.round (lit (float -3.5))))
        (seq
         (prim __autoOutput__ (prim f.round (lit (float 2.4))))
         (seq
          (prim __autoOutput__ (prim f.round (lit (float 2.6))))
          (prim __autoOutput__ (prim f.round (lit (float -0.5))))))))))))) 
 (entry (app (global main))))
